// THE CI STEP THAT RUNS THE DECLARED-ENTRY CALL-SHAPE INSTRUMENT OVER A PR's CHANGED .dag.
//
// gunbc.design_ruling::ci_job_budgets_closed_to_growth is the law this step lives under. It buys a
// check, so it pays for it in a bound, and the bound it pays in is the DIFF: it never walks the
// tree. The whole-tree reading of this instrument was measured, and a scope the size of main's
// tracked .dag is half an hour of resolve, which no job budget of ours carries. It reads what this
// run changed, plus what IMPORTS something this run changed -- and that second half is why the step
// exists at all rather than being a reviewer's habit: the defect this row is named for
// (gunb_ai_pages_workflow passing claim_run to a step that declares no such parameter, discovered
// by a 1391-second / 13.3 GiB CI resolve) was a CALLER NOBODY HAD TO EDIT. A check scoped to
// changed files alone cannot see it. A check scoped to changed files plus their reverse importers sees it
// while the author is still watching the run (93 s on the largest real diff measured here), because the
// caller is in the scope even though no edit touched it.
//
// THE BASE IS THE EVENT'S OWN BASE SHA, NOT main. `git diff --name-only <base>...HEAD`, merge-base
// semantics, with <base> read from the event that carries it: github.event.pull_request.base.sha on
// a pull_request, github.event.merge_group.base_sha on a merge_group. The two alternatives and why
// they fail are the reason this is spelled out rather than left to convention. GITHUB_BASE_REF is a
// BRANCH NAME: it moves under the run, so a main that gains a commit mid-job changes what "the
// diff" means, and two steps of one job would then disagree about the same PR. A two-dot diff
// against main counts main's own new commits as yours, so a PR that changed no .dag at all would be
// charged for someone else's -- and, worse, charged for THEIR break. The event's base sha is the
// commit this run was asked to judge, and it is the only thing that cannot move underneath it.
//
// THE BASE IS FETCHED RATHER THAN ASSUMED. The checkout here is full-depth today, but the step does
// not read that fact: an absent ancestor treated as "nothing changed" is the one silent arm this
// step has, so an unresolvable base is refused out loud. Fetching explicitly also means the step
// keeps its answer if the checkout's depth is ever tuned for speed by someone with other things on
// their mind.
//
// ONLY THE DIFF-CARRYING EVENTS ARE POLICED, and that is a condition on the step rather than an
// arm inside it. A push, a schedule, a dispatch or a workflow_call run carries no base commit: there
// is no PR, and so nothing this run changed that some other run has not already judged -- main's
// own tree is judged by the merge_group runs that produced it. Giving those events an empty diff and
// calling it a pass would be the silent green this row exists to retire; refusing them would make
// every scheduled run red for a reason nobody can act on. A step that does not run, with its
// condition stated, is the honest third thing.
//
// THE STEP AND THE INSTRUMENT SPLIT THE "NOTHING TO DO" CASE, deliberately. The STEP skips on an
// empty diff -- no .dag moved, so nothing this run could have broken -- and prints that it skipped,
// because a step that exits silently on the common path reads as a step that did not run. The
// INSTRUMENT, handed a non-empty scope, refuses a file it cannot read rather than skipping it (the
// header of dag/gunbc/instruments/declared_entry_call_shape.dag carries that argument). The step's
// skip is a judgement about a DIFF, which the step owns; the instrument's skip would be a judgement
// about a FILE it was handed, which it does not.
//
// THE REVERSE-IMPORTER SET COMES FROM git grep -l -F OVER TRACKED .dag AND MAY ONLY OVER-INCLUDE.
// The index answers "which files mention one of these module names", and mention is not import: a
// file that names the module in a comment is a candidate. That is tolerable only because the
// INSTRUMENT, not the index, is the authority on membership -- every candidate is re-read and its
// own import lines re-parsed by the instrument's one parser, and a file that merely mentions the
// stem is filtered there. Narrowing is an optimisation allowed to be wrong in ONE direction: an
// importer the index missed would be a missed refusal, so the stem search is the widest reading of
// the name (a fixed-string match anywhere in the file) and the instrument does the narrowing.
//
// THE CEILING IS A MIS-SCOPE DETECTOR, NOT A PERFORMANCE TUNING. `git diff <base>...HEAD` answered
// with a scope this large means the base is not the ancestor anyone thinks it is -- a squash-merge
// history, a base that resolved to a distant commit -- and at that point the step is not judging
// this run. It refuses, saying so, rather than spending an hour of a 90-minute job on a diff nobody
// asked for. There is deliberately NO FLOOR: a legitimate PR can change one leaf file that nothing
// imports, and a check that reds that PR has bought a false alarm with the safety it sold. The
// instrument's own refusal of an empty scope is the only bottom the step needs.
//
// WHAT THIS MODULE DOES NOT DECIDE, AND WHY THE INVOCATION ARRIVES BY PARAMETER. The binary this job
// runs, the source-root pair it runs it with, and the memory envelope it runs inside belong to the
// job that embeds the step (gunbc.witness.compiler_gate_workflow), which owns those three facts
// elsewhere too; taking them as parameters keeps this file the authority on the DIFF and nothing
// else, and it keeps the lane that witnesses this file resolvable without the CI hub behind those
// facts -- a first draft that imported them here made one claim of this lane take 13 min 54 s to
// resolve, which is the budget law applied to the check that exists to enforce it.
//
// WHY THE BODY IS NODES AND NOT STRING ROWS, AND WHY THAT MATTERS HERE MORE THAN USUALLY. This file
// ships into a REQUIRED job, and the first version of it assembled that job's script from .dag string
// rows -- shell syntax carried as text, with this module owning every quote and brace. That is the
// presumed-scaffold tell gunbc.design_argument::how_to_work names, and it had already cost real defects:
// an `awk` idiom the medium read as string interpolation, a `${name}` the medium read as a reference, and
// a budget guard that quietly disagreed with the instrument's own scope rule. The body is now authored as
// v2.extdeps.languages.bash_build nodes and rendered by v2.workflow.bash_emit, the same route the fleet's
// other node-authored CI runners take, so quoting belongs to one authority and what stays here is the
// order of operations and the refusals. The carrier's own marker says what is left and what retires it.

module gunbc.witness.declared_entry_call_shape_step

import std.types { List, String }
import std.optional { Present }
import std.dissolution { DissolutionCondition, unbound_dissolution, dissolution_description }
import std.disposition { Disposition, Scaffold, SingleAuthority }
import std.decl_ref { DeclarationRef, WholeDeclaration }
import std.algebra { string_contains }
import extdeps.github.actions { Step, RunStep }
import extdeps.languages.yaml.types { yaml_string, kv, YamlKeyValue }
import v2.std.node { Node }
import v2.std.diagnostic { Outcome, Accepted, Rejected, diagnostics_reason_lexemes }
import v2.workflow.bash_emit { bash_emit_stmts }
import v2.extdeps.languages.bash_build {
  BracketQuoted,
  bash_build_assign,
  bash_build_assign_lit,
  bash_build_command_with_words,
  bash_build_exit,
  bash_build_if_else_from_stmts,
  bash_build_if_from_stmts,
  bash_build_negation,
  bash_build_or_else,
  bash_build_pipe,
  bash_build_test_bracket,
  bash_build_with_redir,
  bash_build_with_redir_append_to_file,
  bash_build_with_redir_from_file,
  bash_build_with_redir_stderr_to_stdout,
  bash_build_with_redir_stdout_and_stderr_null,
  bash_build_with_redir_to_file,
  bash_build_word_cmdsubst,
  bash_build_word_concat_from_nodes,
  bash_build_word_exit_status,
  bash_build_word_lit,
  bash_build_word_var
}

// The step's identity. Read by the job's bound-step list and pinned by the lane witness, so this is
// the one spelling of both.
data declared_entry_call_shape_step_name: String = "Declared entry call shape"

data declared_entry_call_shape_step_id: String = "declared-entry-call-shape"

// The instrument this step runs. The step names the FILE and the ENTRY FUNCTION; the instrument
// module owns what it does with them.
data declared_entry_call_shape_instrument_entry: String =
  "dag/gunbc/instruments/declared_entry_call_shape.dag"

data declared_entry_call_shape_entry_function: String = "main"

// The scope files, spelled once each. They are written under `mktemp -d` rather than into the checkout:
// the step is a read of the diff, not a producer, and a step that leaves untracked files in the tree
// makes every later `git status` in the job a question about someone else's artefact. The names are
// consts because the instrument is handed the same two paths the step wrote, and a mismatch between
// what is written and what is passed is a check that silently reads nothing.
data declared_entry_call_shape_changed_name: String = "changed.txt"

data declared_entry_call_shape_candidate_name: String = "candidates.txt"

data declared_entry_call_shape_stems_name: String = "stems.txt"

// THE SCOPE BUDGET, IN THE UNIT THE COST ACTUALLY TRACKS. Measured with the engine built from this
// checkout: a 78-file scope of 53,466 lines -- the shape of a real PR diff here, 11 changed files plus the
// 67 the reverse pass over-included -- scans in 93 s wall, of which ~5 s is the instrument resolving its own
// 53 modules: ~1.65 ms per LINE, ~1.2 s per file. A file count is the wrong ruler, because 600 leaf fixtures
// cost seconds and 600 workflow hubs cost twenty minutes; the line count is what the scanner's time follows.
// 250,000 lines is ~7 minutes at that rate, which fits next to the reference check inside the roster's job
// budget (gunbc.ci.repository_ci_profile.gunbc_roster_gate), and is ~5x the largest scope this row has seen.
// ABOVE IT THE STEP REFUSES RATHER THAN RUNNING PAST THE JOB TIMEOUT, and the remedy is named rather than
// implied: a legitimate tree-wide migration -- which the repo's own replacement-migration rule REQUIRES to be
// one change -- runs this instrument out of band, where its cost is published (~5 s plus ~1.65 ms per line),
// and the run is cited in the PR body. And there is no FLOOR: a legal PR changes one leaf file nothing
// imports, and a check that reds that has bought a false alarm with the safety it sold.
//
// Spelled as text because it is interpolated into the shell test, and a number in a shell test is text
// whatever the language calls it here.
data declared_entry_call_shape_line_ceiling: String = "250000"

// The event's base commit, as the one expression that carries it. `||` is GitHub's fallback: a
// pull_request event has no merge_group payload and a merge_group event has no pull_request payload,
// so exactly one arm resolves on either event and neither on any other -- which is how the step
// learns it is on an event with no diff to police.
data declared_entry_base_sha_expression: String =
  "${{ github.event.pull_request.base.sha || github.event.merge_group.base_sha }}"

// THE ENVIRONMENT NAMES THE EXPRESSIONS BIND INTO. An expression has to reach the workflow document verbatim,
// and the only carrier that guarantees it is a yaml scalar (extdeps.languages.yaml.emit writes an expression
// as it stands); the shell then reads an ordinary variable. Handing one to `bash_build_assign_lit` instead
// fuses two languages in one word -- the serializer's job is to quote and escape it, and GitHub's expression
// grammar admits no backslash -- which is the defect gunbc.required_lanes_gate records at its own
// required_lanes_gate_stmts header: latent until the first expression that needed quoting, because a
// serializer fed only inputs on which two renderings agree has not been tested at all.
data declared_entry_base_env_name: String = "GUNBC_DECLARED_ENTRY_BASE_SHA"

data declared_entry_event_name_env_name: String = "GUNBC_DECLARED_ENTRY_EVENT_NAME"

// THE COMMIT THIS RUN JUDGES, AND WHY THE CHECK NEEDS IT SEPARATELY FROM `HEAD`. On a pull_request run the
// checkout is GitHub's MERGE ref -- the PR head merged onto the base branch as it stood when the ref was
// built -- and the event's `base.sha` is the base branch's tip AT THE TIME OF THE EVENT, which can have moved
// since. Diffing `base...HEAD` over that merge commit then resolves the merge base to the merge's own second
// parent's ancestor and reports MAIN's own new commits as if this PR wrote them: observed on the required
// lane at 3b6ade9f, where a 13-file PR produced a scope of 1,932 .dag files / 905,722 lines and the step's
// line ceiling refused it (the ceiling worked; the scope was wrong). The PR's OWN head sha is the other end
// of the diff the review shows, and `merge-base(base, head)` is the same base `git diff ... A...B` means when
// the history is intact -- which is the merge-base semantics the manager ruled this step to. On a merge_group
// run there is no pull_request payload, the expression renders empty, and `git rev-parse HEAD` (the group's
// result commit) takes its place with merge_group.base_sha as the base: the whole group's change, correctly.
data declared_entry_head_sha_expression: String = "${{ github.event.pull_request.head.sha }}"

data declared_entry_head_env_name: String = "GUNBC_DECLARED_ENTRY_HEAD_SHA"

// The events this step runs on, stated as the step's condition. See the header: the alternative to
// a condition is a silent green on the events that carry no base.
data declared_entry_call_shape_if_condition: String =
  "github.event_name == 'pull_request' || github.event_name == 'merge_group'"

// The CLI verb and the option tokens this invocation passes. These are the spellings the parser in
// v1_compiler.cli_run reads; they are repeated here because 260 .dag files already spell them and no
// module owns them, and minting a private authority for one consumer of a 260-consumer spelling is a
// worse fork than the copy.
data declared_entry_call_shape_verb: String = "run"

data declared_entry_option_entry: String = "--entry"

data declared_entry_option_function: String = "--function"

data declared_entry_option_arg: String = "--arg"

data declared_entry_option_source_root: String = "--source-root"

// The two runner-annotation prefixes. The instrument's own findings reach stderr and the step blocks on its
// exit status; these lines exist so the step's OWN degradations -- a skip, a refusal to judge -- are visible
// in the run rather than only in text someone has to scroll, which is what makes them countable
// (gunbc.design_argument::fail_closed: typed, located and countable). GitHub parses workflow commands from a
// step's stdout, which is why they are echoed unredirected.
data declared_entry_notice_prefix: String = "::notice title=declared entry call shape::"

data declared_entry_error_prefix: String = "::error title=declared entry call shape::"

data declared_entry_changed_arg: String = "changed_paths_file"

data declared_entry_candidates_arg: String = "candidate_importers_file"

data declared_entry_stems_arg: String = "stems_file"

data declared_entry_call_shape_stems_function: String = "declared_entry_call_shape_stems_emit"

// THE CARRIER OF THE `run` VERB. This is the FULL CLI, not the fast-lane's staged binary: the staged
// `v2-native-cli-gen-one` / `gunbc-fast-lane-native-cli` is the native-CLI GENERATOR, whose verbs are
// emit, check-layout, check-references and parse-population -- handed a `run`, it prints
// `REFUSED: unknown verb run -- usage: <binary> emit ...` and exits 2 (observed on the required lane, run
// 46025990627, step "Declared entry call shape"). The full CLI is this job's own "Build the compiler"
// product (`cargo build --release -p v1-compiler --bin gunbc`), the same carrier the job's floor steps
// already invoke at 'target/release/gunbc' and the one the shadow gate names as "the gunbc binary the CI
// builds (same as the required gate)" (hosted_runner_shadow_gate_workflow.dag shadow_gate_gunbc_binary).
data declared_entry_gunbc_bin: String = "target/release/gunbc"

// The event that produced the run, as GitHub renders it into the script. A skip must name its own cause
// in the annotation, and which event arrived is the fact that decided it.
data declared_entry_event_name_expression: String = "${{ github.event_name }}"

// WHETHER THIS JOB BUILT A COMPILER AT ALL, AS THE JOB ALREADY DECIDES IT. `steps.fast_lane.outputs.skip`
// is the job's own answer to "is the materialization reused", written by its "Decide whether the fast-lane
// unit covers this change" step and already consumed the same way by the whole-tree steps that run with the
// restored CLI. Reading that output rather than re-deriving reuse keeps one authority for the question
// (gunbc.design_argument::single_authority); the check only asks it, it never classifies.
data declared_entry_fast_lane_skip_env_name: String = "GUNBC_DECLARED_ENTRY_FAST_LANE_SKIP"

data declared_entry_fast_lane_skip_expression: String = "${{ steps.fast_lane.outputs.skip }}"

data declared_entry_fast_lane_reused_value: String = "true"

// The machine-readable tally a deferral leaves behind. One line, greppable, counting the work the PR lane
// pushed to the queue: without it a deferral is prose, and prose is not countable
// (gunbc.design_argument::fail_closed, "every degradation is typed, located and countable").
data declared_entry_deferral_tally_prefix: String = "judged=0 reason=fast_lane_reused deferred="

// ── THE SCRIPT, AS CONSTRUCTS ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
//
// The body is authored as bash_build NODES and rendered by v2.workflow.bash_emit -- the route the fleet
// already uses for its node-authored runners (gunbc.ci.ci_bash_step gunbc_ci_bash_rendered is the same
// match, and gunbc.machine_intake.mtcollins1_probe_step is the precedent for a whole CI step built this
// way). The first version assembled the script from string rows, which put shell syntax -- `if [ ... ]`,
// `|| {`, `2>/dev/null`, `exit 1` -- inside .dag string literals: the .dag became a container for a program
// written somewhere else, and the quoting of every word was this file's problem to get right by eye. Here a
// word is a word, quoting is the emitter's single authority, and what is left in this file is the ORDER of
// operations and the refusals -- which is the only part that was ever this module's to own.
//
// WHAT THE NODE ROUTE DID NOT RETIRE, AND WHY IT IS SAID OUT LOUD. The sequence of git facts (resolve the
// event's base, fetch it when the checkout does not hold it, take the changed .dag set, narrow the reverse
// importers with `git grep -l -F`) is a policy about a DIFF, and it is stated here as the statements it runs.
// Every JUDGEMENT those statements feed is typed and lives in gunbc.instruments.declared_entry_call_shape:
// which module a path declares, whether a grep hit is a real importer, whether a call passes a label its
// declaration does not name, and whether the scope is empty enough to refuse. What is not yet available as a
// construct is the narrowing itself -- "which tracked file mentions this module name" is a whole-tree content
// search, and the modelled way to ask it (reading every tracked .dag through the effect machine) measured
// 5 m 18 s before it printed anything, against this step's 105 s at a real scope. The marker below names
// that capability as the thing that dissolves the rest.

fn w_lit(text: String) -> Node {
  bash_build_word_lit(text: text)
}

fn w_var(name: String) -> Node {
  bash_build_word_var(name: name)
}

fn cmd(words: List<String>) -> Node {
  bash_build_command_with_words(words: map(words, word => w_lit(word)))
}

// `${dir}/<file>`: one word, built from the variable and the const naming the file, so the path the step
// writes and the path the instrument is handed cannot drift apart by spelling.
fn w_dir_path(name: String) -> Node {
  bash_build_word_concat_from_nodes(parts: [w_var("dir"), w_lit(name)])
}

// `--arg name=${dir}/file`, as ONE word: an option value with an embedded expansion is not three words.
fn w_arg_path(arg: String, name: String) -> Node {
  bash_build_word_concat_from_nodes(parts: [w_lit(concat(arg, "=")), w_var("dir"), w_lit(name)])
}


// One `echo` of one annotation. GitHub parses workflow commands from a step's STDOUT, so an annotation is
// echoed unredirected, as the fleet's own emitted annotations are; the same message sent to stderr is log
// text nobody is required to read, which is the defect the annotation exists to close.
fn cs_announce(prefix: String, message: String) -> Node {
  cs_announce_node(prefix, w_lit(message))
}

// The node form, for the refusals that must NAME THE NUMBERS they judged: the scope and the line count are
// shell expansions, and an expansion inside a word is a concat of literal and variable parts, not a literal
// with a dollar sign in it.
fn cs_announce_node(prefix: String, message: Node) -> Node {
  bash_build_command_with_words(words: [w_lit("echo"), bash_build_word_concat_from_nodes(parts: [w_lit(prefix), message])])
}

fn cs_notice(message: String) -> Node {
  cs_announce(prefix: declared_entry_notice_prefix, message: message)
}

fn cs_refusal(message: String) -> Node {
  cs_announce(prefix: declared_entry_error_prefix, message: message)
}

// One gunbc invocation, in this step's fixed shape: the job's binary, the verb, the job's source-root pair,
// the instrument's file, one of its entries, and the step's own scope files. Both the key pass and the scan
// are this shape, so the two calls cannot drift apart in their options.
fn cs_gunbc_run(bin: String, source_roots: List<String>, entry_function: String, args: List<Node>) -> Node {
  bash_build_command_with_words(words: concat(
    concat(concat([w_lit(bin), w_lit(declared_entry_call_shape_verb)], root_args(source_roots)),
           [w_lit(declared_entry_option_entry), w_lit(declared_entry_call_shape_instrument_entry),
            w_lit(declared_entry_option_function), w_lit(entry_function)]),
    // EVERY SCOPE FILE IS HANDED OVER AS `--arg name=value`: the option token and its value are separate
    // words, and the pair is emitted per argument. A first draft folded the option into each value and
    // shipped `--entry=...` with BARE `changed_paths_file=...` words -- an instrument invoked without its
    // scope files, which is a refusal at the instrument and a red step, so the shape is said once here.
    flat_map(args, arg => [w_lit(declared_entry_option_arg), arg])
  ))
}

fn root_args(source_roots: List<String>) -> List<Node> {
  flat_map(source_roots, root => [w_lit(declared_entry_option_source_root), w_lit(root)])
}

// THE STATEMENTS, in the order they must be true: establish the shell's discipline, take the base from the
// event and REFUSE without one, prepare the scope files, prove the base, take the diff, skip only a diff
// genuinely empty of .dag, ask the instrument for the search keys, narrow with the index, bound the scope,
// and only then run the instrument over what survived.
fn declared_entry_call_shape_scope_statements(
  bin: String,
  source_roots: List<String>
) -> List<Node> {
  [
    cmd(["set", "-euo", "pipefail"]),
    // The base arrives through the step's environment (see the two environment names above); nothing here assigns it.
    bash_build_if_from_stmts(
      cond: bash_build_test_bracket(words: [w_lit("-z"), w_var(declared_entry_base_env_name)], spelling: BracketQuoted),
      then_stmts: [
        cs_announce_node(declared_entry_error_prefix, bash_build_word_concat_from_nodes(parts: [
          w_lit("event "), w_var(declared_entry_event_name_env_name),
          w_lit(" reached this step without a base commit to diff against; the check cannot judge anything and refuses rather than passing an unjudged run")
        ])),
        bash_build_exit(code: "1")
      ],
    ),
    bash_build_assign(name: "dir", value: bash_build_word_cmdsubst(body: cmd(["mktemp", "-d"]))),
    bash_build_assign(name: "changed", value: w_dir_path(declared_entry_call_shape_changed_name)),
    bash_build_assign(name: "candidates", value: w_dir_path(declared_entry_call_shape_candidate_name)),
    bash_build_assign(name: "stems", value: w_dir_path(declared_entry_call_shape_stems_name)),
    // A SHALLOW CHECKOUT CANNOT ANSWER "WHAT DID THIS CHANGE", AND THIS STEP DOES NOT PRETEND IT CAN. The
    // required job checks itself out at `fetch-depth: 0`, so on the lane this arm is silent; it exists because
    // the alternative is the failure mode this step has already been caught in twice. A shallow repository
    // carries grafts with no parents, `git merge-base` cannot cross one, and `A...B` -- the spelling that
    // produced a 1,932-file scope for a 13-file PR on the required lane at 3b6ade9f -- answers with whatever
    // boundary survived. So: if the repository is shallow, try once to make it whole; if it is still shallow,
    // REFUSE with the cause named. A missing base and an unusable history are both refusals here, never skips,
    // and never a scope inferred from what the fetch happened to bring.
    bash_build_assign(name: "shallow", value: bash_build_word_cmdsubst(body: cmd(["git", "rev-parse", "--is-shallow-repository"]))),
    bash_build_if_from_stmts(
      cond: bash_build_test_bracket(words: [w_var("shallow"), w_lit("="), w_lit("true")], spelling: BracketQuoted),
      then_stmts: [
        bash_build_or_else(
          left: bash_build_with_redir_stdout_and_stderr_null(inner: bash_build_command_with_words(words: [w_lit("git"), w_lit("fetch"), w_lit("--no-tags"), w_lit("--unshallow"), w_lit("origin")])),
          right: cmd([":"])
        ),
        bash_build_assign(name: "shallow", value: bash_build_word_cmdsubst(body: cmd(["git", "rev-parse", "--is-shallow-repository"]))),
        bash_build_if_from_stmts(
          cond: bash_build_test_bracket(words: [w_var("shallow"), w_lit("="), w_lit("true")], spelling: BracketQuoted),
          then_stmts: [
            cs_refusal("this checkout is shallow and completing it did not make its history whole, so no merge base can be computed across a shallow graft: refusing to judge a scope the fetch cannot show"),
            bash_build_exit(code: "1")
          ],
        )
      ],
    ),
    // THE BASE IS PROVED, FETCHED, OR THE STEP REFUSES. An ancestor that is simply not there must never be
    // allowed to answer "nothing changed": that is the one arm that could turn a broken run into a green.
    bash_build_if_from_stmts(
      cond: bash_build_negation(inner: bash_build_with_redir(
        inner: bash_build_command_with_words(words: [w_lit("git"), w_lit("cat-file"), w_lit("-e"), bash_build_word_concat_from_nodes(parts: [w_var(declared_entry_base_env_name), w_lit("^"), w_lit("{"), w_lit("commit}")])]),
        redir: ^bash_production_fold_redir_stderr_null
      )),
      then_stmts: [
        // FETCHED AT FULL DEPTH, AND THAT IS THE POINT. `--depth=1` was the first draft's spelling, and it is
        // the defect: a depth-limited fetch grafts the fetched commit as a PARENTLESS root, so the merge base
        // between it and HEAD cannot be computed -- git either says "no merge base" (reproduced locally in a
        // depth-1 clone: `fatal: <base>...HEAD: no merge base`, exit 128, an untyped death under set -e) or
        // resolves it to whatever shallow boundary survived and hands the check MAIN's history as this PR's
        // scope. Correctness of the scope outranks the transfer size of an arm that, on this job's own
        // `fetch-depth: 0` checkout, never runs at all.
        bash_build_if_from_stmts(
          cond: bash_build_negation(inner: bash_build_with_redir_stdout_and_stderr_null(inner: bash_build_command_with_words(words: [w_lit("git"), w_lit("fetch"), w_lit("--no-tags"), w_lit("origin"), w_var(declared_entry_base_env_name)]))),
          then_stmts: [
            cs_refusal("the event base commit carried by GUNBC_DECLARED_ENTRY_BASE_SHA is not in this checkout and could not be fetched: refusing to guess a scope"),
            bash_build_exit(code: "1")
          ],
        )
      ],
    ),
    // THE OTHER END OF THE DIFF: the PR's own head, falling back to the checked-out commit when the event
    // carries none (a merge_group run), and proved in the checkout by the same full-depth rule.
    bash_build_assign(name: "head", value: w_var(declared_entry_head_env_name)),
    bash_build_if_from_stmts(
      cond: bash_build_test_bracket(words: [w_lit("-z"), w_var("head")], spelling: BracketQuoted),
      then_stmts: [
        bash_build_assign(name: "head", value: bash_build_word_cmdsubst(body: cmd(["git", "rev-parse", "HEAD"])))
      ],
    ),
    bash_build_if_from_stmts(
      cond: bash_build_negation(inner: bash_build_with_redir(
        inner: bash_build_command_with_words(words: [w_lit("git"), w_lit("cat-file"), w_lit("-e"), bash_build_word_concat_from_nodes(parts: [w_var("head"), w_lit("^"), w_lit("{"), w_lit("commit}")])]),
        redir: ^bash_production_fold_redir_stderr_null
      )),
      then_stmts: [
        bash_build_if_from_stmts(
          cond: bash_build_negation(inner: bash_build_with_redir_stdout_and_stderr_null(inner: bash_build_command_with_words(words: [w_lit("git"), w_lit("fetch"), w_lit("--no-tags"), w_lit("origin"), w_var("head")]))),
          then_stmts: [
            cs_refusal("the commit this run is supposed to judge is not in this checkout and could not be fetched: refusing to guess a scope"),
            bash_build_exit(code: "1")
          ],
        )
      ],
    ),
    // THE MERGE BASE, COMPUTED AND REFUSED ON, NEVER INFERRED BY THE DIFF COMMAND. `A...B` asks git for the
    // same thing; asking first is what turns "no merge base" from a bare `fatal:` under `set -e` into a
    // located annotation naming what is missing and why the check will not guess.
    bash_build_if_from_stmts(
      cond: bash_build_negation(inner: bash_build_with_redir_stdout_and_stderr_null(inner: bash_build_command_with_words(words: [w_lit("git"), w_lit("merge-base"), w_var(declared_entry_base_env_name), w_var("head")]))),
      then_stmts: [
        cs_refusal("the event base commit and the commit this run judges share no merge base, so the diff has no honest starting point: refusing rather than judging a scope invented from a shallow or unrelated history"),
        bash_build_exit(code: "1")
      ],
    ),
    bash_build_assign(name: "merge_base", value: bash_build_word_cmdsubst(body: bash_build_command_with_words(words: [w_lit("git"), w_lit("merge-base"), w_var(declared_entry_base_env_name), w_var("head")]))),
    // DELETED FILES DO NOT JOIN THE SCOPE (`--diff-filter=d`): a path this change removed has no call shape
    // left to read, and handing the instrument a path that is not on disk turns a real green into a read error.
    bash_build_with_redir_to_file(
      inner: bash_build_command_with_words(words: [w_lit("git"), w_lit("diff"), w_lit("--name-only"), w_lit("--diff-filter=d"), w_var("merge_base"), w_var("head"), w_lit("--"), w_lit("*.dag")]),
      path: w_var("changed")
    ),
    bash_build_if_from_stmts(
      cond: bash_build_test_bracket(words: [w_lit("!"), w_lit("-s"), w_var("changed")], spelling: BracketQuoted),
      then_stmts: [
        cs_notice("skipped: no .dag change in this diff, scope empty"),
        bash_build_exit(code: "0")
      ],
    ),
    // THE FAST LANE MAY HOLD THE TOOLCHAIN, AND THE LANE THAT CLOSES THIS CANNOT SKIP.
    // `Build the compiler` is gated on `steps.fast_lane.outputs.skip != 'true'`: when the job's own classify
    // step reuses the materialization it builds no compiler, and the only CLI that run has is the restored
    // native CLI -- whose verbs are emit, check-layout, check-references and parse-population, no `run` (the
    // job says so itself: "the whole-tree checks run with the native CLI main built at that commit (REUSED,
    // not fresh)"). So a reused run holds nothing that can execute this instrument, whatever the diff says.
    // That is a MISSING TOOL, not a finding about the diff, and failing the required job for it would put a
    // defect of the pipeline on every author whose PR touches .dag outside the compiler's read set -- the
    // majority of the population this check exists to judge. So the deferral is honest rather than fatal, and
    // honest in the way fail_closed requires: TYPED (its own notice, naming the cause and the tool that was
    // absent), LOCATED (this arm, between the empty-scope skip and the carrier preflight -- it fires only
    // when there is real work and no way to do it), COUNTABLE (the tally line, naming how much was deferred).
    // AND IT CLAIMS NO CLOSER IT DOES NOT HAVE. Two candidates were argued here and both are false as written,
    // so neither is said anywhere in the emitted script -- there are claims refusing them. (a) The merge queue:
    // the workflow declares `merge_group`, but the last 200 witnesses runs held none (184 pull_request, 16
    // push), so the queue does not run. (b) The push run on main: it never reuses the fast lane and always
    // builds, which sounds like the closer -- except this step is enrolled only on the events that carry a base
    // commit, which is what refuses an unjudged run, and a `push` carries none. So main builds a compiler this
    // step never asks. The honest statement is that in this population nothing judges the diff today; what ends
    // that is the fast-lane unit carrying a run-verb CLI, named in the annotation by its row id
    // (remote-build-fast-lane-seed-cli), with enrolling the push lane on a base it can compute as that row's
    // second half. Design note §16 carries the same account.
    bash_build_if_from_stmts(
      cond: bash_build_test_bracket(words: [
        w_var(declared_entry_fast_lane_skip_env_name), w_lit("="), w_lit(declared_entry_fast_lane_reused_value)
      ], spelling: BracketQuoted),
      then_stmts: [
        cs_announce_node(prefix: declared_entry_notice_prefix, message: bash_build_word_concat_from_nodes(parts: [
          w_lit("deferred: the fast lane was reused, so this job built no compiler and no CLI here can run the instrument, and no other event enrolls this step -- nothing judges these "),
          w_var("scope"), w_lit(" .dag files on this run. The fast-lane unit carrying a run-verb CLI closes this (roadmap: remote-build-fast-lane-seed-cli)")
        ])),
        bash_build_command_with_words(words: [w_lit("echo"), bash_build_word_concat_from_nodes(parts: [
          w_lit(declared_entry_deferral_tally_prefix), w_var("scope"), w_lit(" .dag files, "), w_var("lines"), w_lit(" lines")
        ])]),
        bash_build_exit(code: "0")
      ],
    ),
    // THE CARRIER MUST EXIST BEFORE IT IS BLAMED -- when the job claimed it would build one. A full run that
    // reaches here with no run-capable CLI is a contradiction inside the job itself: `Build the compiler`
    // said it would run and its product is not there. That is refused, not skipped; a check that passes when
    // it could not run is the green this row exists to end (gunbc.design_argument::fail_closed).
    bash_build_if_from_stmts(
      cond: bash_build_negation(inner: bash_build_command_with_words(words: [w_lit("test"), w_lit("-x"), w_lit(bin)])),
      then_stmts: [
        cs_refusal(concat("no executable run-verb CLI at ", concat(bin, ": this is not a reused-fast-lane run, so the job promised a compiler build and this diff carries .dag changes for the check to judge. Re-run, or carry the CLI in the fast-lane cache"))),
        bash_build_exit(code: "1")
      ],
    ),
    // THE SEARCH KEYS ARE THE INSTRUMENT'S. Which module a changed path declares is cs_module_of_file's rule,
    // and a second spelling of it -- the first version re-derived the keys with `sed` -- had a silently
    // available disagreement: keys the grep matched nothing for, an empty scope, and a green that had judged
    // none of the reverse importers. The entry refuses its own empty key set.
    cs_gunbc_run(
      bin: bin,
      source_roots: source_roots,
      entry_function: declared_entry_call_shape_stems_function,
      args: [
        w_arg_path(declared_entry_changed_arg, declared_entry_call_shape_changed_name),
        w_arg_path(declared_entry_stems_arg, declared_entry_call_shape_stems_name)
      ]
    ),
    bash_build_with_redir_to_file(inner: cmd([":"]), path: w_var("candidates")),
    bash_build_if_from_stmts(
      cond: bash_build_test_bracket(words: [w_lit("-s"), w_var("stems")], spelling: BracketQuoted),
      then_stmts: [
        // `git grep` exits 1 on 'no match', which is an answer, and >=2 on a real error, which is not: the
        // two are told apart rather than swallowed together. The grep sits in a CONDITION so `set -e` does
        // not turn the ordinary no-match answer into the step's death.
        bash_build_if_else_from_stmts(
          cond: bash_build_with_redir_to_file(
            inner: bash_build_command_with_words(words: [w_lit("git"), w_lit("grep"), w_lit("-l"), w_lit("-F"), w_lit("-f"), w_var("stems"), w_lit("--"), w_lit("*.dag")]),
            path: bash_build_word_concat_from_nodes(parts: [w_var("candidates"), w_lit(".grep")])
          ),
          then_stmts: [cmd([":"])],
          else_stmts: [
            bash_build_assign(name: "rc", value: bash_build_word_exit_status()),
            bash_build_if_from_stmts(
              cond: bash_build_test_bracket(words: [w_var("rc"), w_lit("-gt"), w_lit("1")], spelling: BracketQuoted),
              then_stmts: [
                cs_refusal("git grep refused the reverse-import search: this is a repository error, not an empty answer"),
                bash_build_exit(code: "1")
              ],
            )
          ]
        ),
        bash_build_with_redir_append_to_file(
          inner: bash_build_command_with_words(words: [w_lit("cat"), bash_build_word_concat_from_nodes(parts: [w_var("candidates"), w_lit(".grep")])]),
          path: w_var("candidates")
        )
      ],
    ),
    bash_build_command_with_words(words: [w_lit("sort"), w_lit("-u"), w_var("changed"), w_var("candidates"), w_lit("-o"), w_var("candidates")]),
    bash_build_assign(name: "scope", value: bash_build_word_cmdsubst(body: bash_build_pipe(
      left: bash_build_with_redir_from_file(inner: cmd(["wc", "-l"]), path: w_var("candidates")),
      right: cmd(["tr", "-d", " "])
    ))),
    // The cost model, measured: ~1.65 ms per line. The budget is therefore counted in LINES, here, where the
    // file list is -- and one `cat` joined by one `wc -l` avoids both the multi-file "total" line and the
    // awk idiom the string medium could not spell.
    bash_build_assign_lit(name: "rc", text: "0"),
    bash_build_or_else(
      left: bash_build_assign(name: "lines", value: bash_build_word_cmdsubst(body: bash_build_pipe(
        left: bash_build_pipe(
          left: bash_build_command_with_words(words: [w_lit("xargs"), w_lit("-r"), bash_build_word_concat_from_nodes(parts: [w_lit("-a"), w_var("candidates")]), w_lit("cat")]),
          right: cmd(["wc", "-l"])
        ),
        right: cmd(["tr", "-d", " "])
      ))),
      right: bash_build_assign(name: "rc", value: bash_build_word_exit_status())
    ),
    // THE BUDGET IS COUNTED AGAINST A SCOPE THAT WAS READ. `pipefail` is on, so a candidate the reader could
    // not open fails the pipeline; that status is taken rather than letting `set -e` kill the step with no
    // annotation, and it is refused -- the alternative is to compare an unknown scope against the ceiling and
    // call the difference a pass (gunbc.design_argument::fail_closed, "runtime uncertainty -> typed refusal").
    bash_build_if_from_stmts(
      cond: bash_build_test_bracket(words: [w_var("rc"), w_lit("-gt"), w_lit("0")], spelling: BracketQuoted),
      then_stmts: [
        cs_refusal("the line count of the scope could not be computed: a candidate file could not be read. Refusing rather than budgeting against a scope that was not read"),
        bash_build_exit(code: "1")
      ],
    ),
    bash_build_if_from_stmts(
      cond: bash_build_test_bracket(words: [w_var("lines"), w_lit("-gt"), w_lit(declared_entry_call_shape_line_ceiling)], spelling: BracketQuoted),
      then_stmts: [
        cs_announce_node(declared_entry_error_prefix, bash_build_word_concat_from_nodes(parts: [
          w_lit("scope of "), w_var("scope"), w_lit(" .dag files / "), w_var("lines"),
          w_lit(" lines is above the budget of "), w_lit(declared_entry_call_shape_line_ceiling),
          w_lit(" lines: this is not this run's diff, and the step refuses to judge it. A tree-wide migration runs this instrument out of band and cites the run in the PR body")
        ])),
        bash_build_exit(code: "1")
      ],
    ),
    // THE SCOPE THE STEP ACTUALLY COMPUTED, printed before the instrument speaks: the numbers its own counts
    // line will be read against, and the one line that makes a mis-scoped run visible rather than merely green.
    bash_build_command_with_words(words: [w_lit("echo"), bash_build_word_concat_from_nodes(parts: [
      w_lit("scope: "), w_var("scope"), w_lit(" .dag files, "), w_var("lines"),
      w_lit(" lines (changed plus reverse importers, diffed from the merge base "), w_var("merge_base"), w_lit(" of the event base "), w_var(declared_entry_base_env_name),
      w_lit(", budget "), w_lit(declared_entry_call_shape_line_ceiling), w_lit(")")
    ])]),
    cs_gunbc_run(
      bin: bin,
      source_roots: source_roots,
      entry_function: declared_entry_call_shape_entry_function,
      args: [
        w_arg_path(declared_entry_changed_arg, declared_entry_call_shape_changed_name),
        w_arg_path(declared_entry_candidates_arg, declared_entry_call_shape_candidate_name)
      ]
    )
  ]
}

// THE RENDERING, through the emitter that owns shell syntax -- and a refusal to render becomes poison, never
// a partial script. Poison on rejection is the rule the fleet's other emitted carriers use: a step whose
// script could not be built must make the committed workflow red, because a step that silently checks nothing
// is exactly the green this row was written to end.
data declared_entry_call_shape_emit_refused_poison: String = "__GUNBC_ORCH_EMIT_REFUSED__ gunbc declared-entry call shape step emission was rejected by v2.workflow.bash_emit: the scope statement tree could not be rendered. This literal must never reach a committed workflow; the gate below refuses instead."

fn declared_entry_call_shape_script_outcome(
  bin: String,
  source_roots: List<String>
) -> Outcome<String> {
  bash_emit_stmts(statements: declared_entry_call_shape_scope_statements(bin: bin, source_roots: source_roots), set_e: false)
}

fn declared_entry_call_shape_script(
  bin: String,
  source_roots: List<String>
) -> String {
  match declared_entry_call_shape_script_outcome(bin: bin, source_roots: source_roots) {
    Accepted { value: script, diagnostics: _ } => script
    // The reason travels with the poison. A carrier that says only "rejected" sends the next reader to
    // the emitter's source to learn what it refused; the diagnostic lexemes name it in the log line.
    Rejected { diagnostics: diagnostics } => concat(
      declared_entry_call_shape_emit_refused_poison,
      concat(" reason: ", diagnostics_reason_lexemes(diagnostics: diagnostics))
    )
  }
}

// THE GATE, so a refusal to render stops the artifact rather than shipping it. Checked by the lane witness on
// the rendered script, which is the path a PR actually walks: an unrenderable statement tree reddens the claim
// before it can reddens a runner.
fn declared_entry_call_shape_script_is_renderable(
  bin: String,
  source_roots: List<String>
) -> Bool {
  match declared_entry_call_shape_script_outcome(bin: bin, source_roots: source_roots) {
    Accepted { value: script, diagnostics: _ } =>
      !string_contains(s: script, pattern: "__GUNBC_ORCH_EMIT_REFUSED__")
        && string_contains(s: script, pattern: "git diff --name-only")
        && string_contains(s: script, pattern: "exit 1")
    Rejected { diagnostics: _ } => false
  }
}

// THE SCAFFOLD MARKER, in the form the fleet's other emitted carriers use (a sibling of
// gunbc.witness.compiler_gate_contract_stamp's pair, and of gunbc.machine_intake.mtcollins1_probe_step's): the
// Disposition names what the artifact is and what it binds to, and the DissolutionCondition names the
// capability that retires the rest of it. Both are on the carrier, and the marker line rides on the FIRST
// line of the emitted script so the committed YAML carries the obligation too.
data declared_entry_call_shape_shell_emit_scaffold: Disposition = Scaffold {
  dissolves_to: SingleAuthority,
  bind: DeclarationRef {
    module_path: "gunbc.witness.declared_entry_call_shape_step",
    decl_name: "declared_entry_call_shape_scope_statements",
    field: WholeDeclaration
  }
}

data declared_entry_call_shape_shell_emit_dissolution_trigger: DissolutionCondition = unbound_dissolution(description: "dissolve-on: declared_entry_call_shape_scope_statements -- the node-authored carrier of the git facts this step needs (event base resolution and fetch, the changed .dag diff, and the `git grep -l -F` narrowing for the reverse-import pass), rendered by v2.workflow.bash_emit bash_emit_stmts, so the shell syntax itself is already the model's; the residual is the NARROWING, which is a whole-tree content search the model can only answer by reading every tracked .dag (measured 5 m 18 s, against this step's 105 s at a real scope). DISSOLVES WHEN a modelled whole-tree content search exists that fits the required-job budget -- at which point the reverse-import pass moves into gunbc.instruments.declared_entry_call_shape, these statements shrink to the diff and the invocation, and the scope policy joins the instrument that already owns every judgement made about a candidate file.")

// THE MARKER, first line of the emitted script, as a comment: it costs the runner nothing, changes no
// behaviour, and is what a reader of the committed YAML -- or a reviewer grepping it -- finds first. Checked
// by the lane witness on the rendered step, so a refactor that drops it reddens a claim rather than quietly
// un-marking a carrier.
fn declared_entry_call_shape_marker_line() -> String {
  concat("# ", concat(dissolution_description(condition: declared_entry_call_shape_shell_emit_dissolution_trigger), "\n"))
}

// THE STEP'S TWO ENVIRONMENT BINDINGS, and the only place either expression is emitted. A yaml scalar is the
// one carrier that promises a GitHub expression reaches the document verbatim; the script reads them as
// GUNBC_DECLARED_ENTRY_BASE_SHA / GUNBC_DECLARED_ENTRY_EVENT_NAME. See the comment on the names above and
// gunbc.required_lanes_gate.required_lanes_gate_stmts for the defect this seam closes.
fn declared_entry_call_shape_env() -> List<YamlKeyValue> {
  [
    kv(key: declared_entry_base_env_name, value: yaml_string(s: declared_entry_base_sha_expression)),
    kv(key: declared_entry_event_name_env_name, value: yaml_string(s: declared_entry_event_name_expression)),
    kv(key: declared_entry_head_env_name, value: yaml_string(s: declared_entry_head_sha_expression)),
    kv(key: declared_entry_fast_lane_skip_env_name, value: yaml_string(s: declared_entry_fast_lane_skip_expression))
  ]
}

fn declared_entry_call_shape_step(
  bin: String,
  source_roots: List<String>
) -> Step {
  RunStep {
    name: Present { value: declared_entry_call_shape_step_name },
    id: Present { value: declared_entry_call_shape_step_id },
    run: concat(declared_entry_call_shape_marker_line(), declared_entry_call_shape_script(bin: bin, source_roots: source_roots)),
    shell: none,
    // NO ENVELOPE INPUT, ON PURPOSE. The envelope requirement this job sets on its subject steps
    // (GUNBC_MEMORY_ENVELOPE_REQUIRED=slot) is read by the TEST route -- v1_compiler.cli_run.target_invocation_host
    // test_verb_checked, the same file that declares MEMORY_ENVELOPE_REQUIRED_ENV -- and not by `gunbc run`,
    // which is what this step invokes. Carrying the input would put a line in the shipped YAML asserting a
    // bound the verb never takes: a receipt for a measurement nobody made. What bounds this step is its scope,
    // and what it costs is measured with the engine built from this checkout: 53 modules resolved in ~5 s at a
    // peak of ~178 MiB, and a 78-file / 53,779-line scope scanned in ~105 s (~1.65 ms per line). See the
    // design note handoff/2026-11-26/gunbc-entries-resolve-checked-in-ci-design.md.
    env: Present { value: declared_entry_call_shape_env() },
    working_directory: none,
    if_condition: Present { value: declared_entry_call_shape_if_condition },
    continue_on_error: none,
    timeout_minutes: none
  }
}
